## Anti-seizure medicines

Medicines such as **lamotrigine** and **levetiracetam** are commonly used. The choice depends on your type of seizures, age, and whether you could become pregnant.

- take them **at the same times every day**
- do not stop suddenly, as this can trigger seizures
- tell your specialist about side effects, as a different medicine may suit you better

## Sodium valproate

**Sodium valproate** is effective, but it can seriously harm an unborn baby. It must **not** be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there is no other suitable option. Women and girls who could get pregnant must be on a **pregnancy prevention programme**. Do not stop it without talking to your doctor.

## Emergency medicine

If you have long or repeated seizures, you may be prescribed **rescue medicine**, such as **buccal midazolam**, that a trained person can give.

## If medicines do not work

- **surgery** to remove the part of the brain causing seizures
- **vagus nerve stimulation**, a device fitted under the skin of the chest
- a **ketogenic diet**, mainly for children, with specialist support

You should have a review with your GP or specialist at least once a year.
